  "query_preview": "..(This Evening Through Saturday)\nIssued at 106 PM MDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery shows shortwave trough pushing through \neastern and central Wyoming this afternoon. Northwesterly low \nlevel flow persists behind a surface cold front that stretches \nfrom eastern ND through eastern SD into central NE. Isolated \nconvection initiated this morning across NE WY with additional \nstorms expected through the afternoon and evening across NE WY and\nthe far southwestern portion of the forecast area. MLCAPE values \n~250-750 J/kg will be in place this afternoon and evening, \nalthough effective shear will be limited (~30-35 knots or less). A\nfew storms may be strong to severe (Marginal risk across portions\nof NE WY and SW SD), although overall instability and shear \nparameter space will preclude more organized severe convection.\n\nUpper level ridge flattens Sunday as upper level flow turns zonal \nthen northwesterly into early next week. Ample instability will \nbe in place Sunday with effective shear around 35 knots, \nsupporting threat of transient strong to severe storms. However, \nupper level ascent will be weak, limiting extent of the severe \nthreat Sunday afternoon and evening. Best chance for convection \nSunday afternoon will be across the Black Hills (central and \nsouthern) given orographic lift and across NW SD where better \nupper level ascent will be present.\n\nGenerally westerly to northwesterly flow will persist through \nmid-week with near-daily chances of thunderstorms. Severe threat \nMonday is still uncertain and contingent upon timing of the upper \nwave and surface front. Sufficient shear is in place Monday \n(effective bulk shear 35-50+ knots) for more organized severe \nconvection should the other ingredients fall into place.\n\nStrong upper ridge is forecast to build over the southwestern US \nlate next week, spreading into the Rockies and Northern Plains. \nExpect temperatures to warm back into the upper 80s to 90s to end \nthe week into next weekend.",
